  "text": "The study of language, culture, and society can be approached from many angles. Traditional scholars of ancient South Asian texts and modern social researchers often examine overlapping material, yet their different backgrounds have kept them apart. The former tend to view the latter as focusing on remote mountain communities, local folk beliefs, and current issues, while the latter see the former as preoccupied with grammatical details and obscure philosophical doctrines. Despite this, each discipline relies on concepts the other traditionally handles; for example, social researchers discuss language, and text scholars discuss culture. A recent case is the idea of a cultural shift toward the norms of the ancient liturgical language, a term coined by social researchers that clearly references that language. Drawing on training in both fields, I share some thoughts on this cultural shift, hoping to address the concerns of social researchers."
